winewayland: give a client surface's viewport a destination before anything can present

Premiere's Program Monitor was grossly oversized on the FIRST fullscreen entry -- it spilled onto
the neighbouring monitor and past the bottom of the desktop. The second and every later entry were
correct, which is what made it look random.

CAUSE. wp_viewport_set_destination() is what maps a client surface's buffer (device pixels) down to
its logical size. It was only ever set from wayland_surface_reconfigure_client(), which runs when
the client surface is ATTACHED -- and a GL client can present before that. With no destination the
compositor shows the buffer 1:1, i.e. scale-times too large.

MEASURED, from an ordinary +waylanddrv trace of a fullscreen entry:

    4485.006  wayland_opengl_surface_create      GL drawable created
    4485.037  wayland_drawable_flush             FIRST PRESENT -- no destination yet
    4485.046  reconfigure_client 2259x1271       destination set, 9 ms too late

A 3840 px buffer shown 1:1 on a 2259-wide logical output is 1.7x oversized. Only the first entry is
affected because afterwards the viewport already carries a destination.

FIX: set the destination in wayland_client_surface_create(), immediately after the viewport exists,
from the window's own client rect. reconfigure_client refreshes it a few ms later as usual; the
point is that it is never UNSET while a buffer is live.

⭐ AND IT MUST BE THE COMPOSITOR'S SCALE, NOT raw_dpi/96. The first version of this used
dpi/96 = 163/96 = 1.69792 and computed 2262x1272, which still overhung the output by 3 px --
operator-visible as a few pixels spilling onto the next monitor. wp_fractional_scale quantises to
1/120, so round(163 * 1.25) = 204 and 204/120 = 1.7 exactly, giving 3840*120/204 = 2259. That
agrees with what reconfigure_client computes, so the initial value and the refresh no longer
disagree. Operator-confirmed: correctly sized on the first fullscreen, no spill.

⚠️ STILL OPEN (separate, same first-entry window): the GL surface is presented ONCE at creation
before the application has rendered into it, so the first fullscreen can come up BLACK. Same shape
as the splash bug fixed in zzzzzzzzzzt -- publishing a buffer the app has not drawn into. The
oversize and the black were two faces of that single unconfigured first present; this patch fixes
the geometry half only.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -2783,6 +2783,51 @@
     client->wp_viewport =
         wp_viewporter_get_viewport(process_wayland.wp_viewporter,
                                     client->wl_surface);
+    /* NEUTRON: give the viewport a destination IMMEDIATELY.
+     *
+     * The destination is what maps the buffer (device pixels) down to the surface's logical size.
+     * It was only ever set from wayland_surface_reconfigure_client(), which runs when the client
+     * surface is attached -- and a GL client can PRESENT BEFORE THAT. With no destination the
+     * compositor shows the buffer 1:1, i.e. scale-times too large.
+     *
+     * MEASURED 2026-08-31, Premiere's Program Monitor going fullscreen:
+     *     4485.006  GL drawable created
+     *     4485.037  wayland_drawable_flush          <- first present, NO destination yet
+     *     4485.046  reconfigure_client 2259x1271    <- destination set, 9 ms too late
+     * A 3840 px buffer shown 1:1 on a 2259-wide logical output spilled onto the second monitor and
+     * past the bottom. Only the FIRST fullscreen entry is affected -- afterwards the viewport
+     * already has a destination -- which is exactly what the operator reported.
+     *
+     * The scale here is derived the same way the surface's own scale is (raw monitor DPI / 96),
+     * so this matches what reconfigure_client will compute; it is a correct value, not a guess,
+     * and is refreshed by the normal path a few ms later regardless. */
+    if (client->wp_viewport)
+    {
+        UINT dpi = NtUserGetWinMonitorDpi(hwnd, MDT_RAW_DPI);
+        RECT r = {0};
+
+        if (dpi && NtUserGetClientRect(hwnd, &r, dpi))
+        {
+            /* Quantise to the 1/120 steps wp_fractional_scale uses, or we land a few px out.
+             * MEASURED: dpi 163 gives 163/96 = 1.69792 and 3840/1.69792 = 2262, but the
+             * compositor's scale is exactly 1.7 and its logical width is 2259 -- a 3 px overhang
+             * that spilled onto the neighbouring monitor. round(163 * 1.25) = 204, 204/120 = 1.7,
+             * and 3840 * 120 / 204 = 2259 exactly. Same arithmetic the surface itself ends up
+             * using, so this initial value now AGREES with what reconfigure_client computes. */
+            int scale120 = (dpi * 120 + 48) / 96;
+            int w = scale120 ? ((r.right - r.left) * 120 + scale120 / 2) / scale120 : 0;
+            int h = scale120 ? ((r.bottom - r.top) * 120 + scale120 / 2) / scale120 : 0;
+
+            if (w > 0 && h > 0)
+            {
+                wp_viewport_set_destination(client->wp_viewport, w, h);
+                ERR("neutron-vpinit: hwnd=%p initial viewport destination %dx%d (buffer %ldx%ld, "
+                    "dpi %u, scale120 %d) -- without this the first present is shown 1:1\n",
+                    hwnd, w, h, (long)(r.right - r.left), (long)(r.bottom - r.top), dpi, scale120);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
     if (!client->wp_viewport)
     {
         ERR("Failed to create client wp_viewport\n");
